<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1694">1694</a>–<a href="/years/1728">1728</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold, Prince of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> from 1704 (ruling in person from 1715) to 1728, was a Calvinist ruler of a small German principality and a keen amateur musician. He is remembered for employing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a> from 1717 to 1723, when Bach composed much of his instrumental and chamber music, including works later gathered as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-concertos">Brandenburg Concertos</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-prince-of-anhalt-kothen.jpg" alt="Leopold, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leopold_von_Anhalt-K%C3%B6then_(1694_-_1728).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leopold was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> on 29 November 1694, the eldest surviving son of Emmanuel Lebrecht, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen, and Gisela Agnes of Rath. Because his parents' marriage was morganatic, his right to inherit was confirmed only on 28 June 1698. When his father died, his mother acted as regent, while King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-prussia">Frederick I of Prussia</a> was his upper guardian. The two quarrelled over his upbringing: the king wanted a Reformed education, his Lutheran mother her own faith, and in 1708 he was sent to the Knight's Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel">Brandenburg an der Havel</a>.</p><p>His Grand Tour, begun on 9 October 1710, took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, where the operas of Jean-Baptiste Lully began his lifelong love of opera, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. He learned the harpsichord and violin and spent 55,749 Thalers on his travels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1714 the dissolution of the royal Prussian court orchestra let him found a court Kapelle, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> musicians entered his service under the opera composer Augustin Reinhard Stricker. Declared of age on 30 November 1715, he forced his younger brother Augustus Louis to give up joint rule under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a> adopted in 1702. Disputes followed with his brother and his mother; in 1721 he sent troops into her estate at Nienburg, and the brothers concluded a final division treaty in August 1722.</p><p>Leopold probably met Bach in January 1716 at his sister's wedding. The plain church music of his Reformed court turned Bach to instrumental and secular vocal works, among them Part I of the Well-Tempered Clavier. The prince, who played the viola da gamba, stood godfather to Bach's son Leopold Augustus. Bach left in 1723 mainly because Leopold, paying more to the Prussian military, reduced the orchestra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leopold's only son died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in August 1728 and a daughter in September. The prince caught the disease, played his violin for the last time on 17 November and died two days later, aged thirty-three. His brother Augustus Louis succeeded him. Bach, who had kept the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a> court composer, directed the funeral music in 1729; its text survives but the music is lost.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leopold, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its court is best known for Prince <b>Leopold</b>, a music lover who employed Johann Sebastian Bach as Kapellmeister from 1717 to 1723, when the Brandenburg Concertos and much chamber music were composed.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-kothen">Principality of Anhalt-Köthen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.75,11.97&amp;year=1716" aria-label="Köthen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 374 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leopold, Prince of Anhalt-Köthen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.75,11.97&amp;year=1716">What was here in 1716? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
